{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "154",
    "case_summary": "A 54-year-old fisherman with no significant medical history was brought to the emergency department after a rapid ascent from a 50 m sea‐depth saturation dive. He complained of dyspnea, lethargy, severe generalized arthralgia, and mild confusion. He was alert with a Glasgow Coma Scale score of 14. Physical examination showed bilateral joint pain worsened by movement without erythema or swelling; neurological, chest, cardiac, and abdominal examinations were otherwise unremarkable. Vital signs were blood pressure 90/60 mm Hg, pulse 95 bpm, respiratory rate 24/min, oxygen saturation 90% on room air, and temperature 36.6 °C. The Wells pulmonary embolism score was 3.\n\nHigh‐flow oxygen was started. An electrocardiogram was normal. Posterior–anterior chest radiography showed no abnormalities. Laboratory tests revealed a white-cell count of 13 × 10^9/L, C-reactive protein 73 mg/L, and otherwise normal hemoglobin, platelets, glucose, ESR, creatinine, urea, potassium, sodium, and D-dimer. Arterial blood gas analysis demonstrated metabolic acidosis with pH 7.32, PCO2 28 mm Hg, HCO3– 15 mmol/L, and PO2 63 mm Hg. Transthoracic echocardiography showed normal biventricular size and function and no valvular disease; however, spontaneous echo contrast consistent with microbubbles was seen in the right heart chambers. A Valsalva maneuver–enhanced color‐flow Doppler study showed no evidence of a patent foramen ovale.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Decompression sickness\nB. DCS\nC. Pneumothorax was excluded\nD. Aspiration pneumonia was excluded\nE. Pulmonary embolism was excluded\nF. Decompression sickness was considered\nG. None\nH. None\n",
